    <description>WHAT IT DOES: Records how two people are related, in both directions, using Nonprofit Cloud's Contact Contact Relations. Give it the two Contacts and the relationship in plain words ("Mother", "Spouse or Partner", "Child") and it writes both sides: Layla is Omar's Spouse, and Omar is Layla's Spouse.

HOW IT WORKS, STEP BY STEP:
1. Translates the everyday word into a Party Role Relation name: every kind of parent (Mother, Father, Stepparent, Legal Guardian, Foster Parent) becomes Parent, a partner becomes Spouse, anything else passes through as-is. Party Role Relations are org data you manage in Setup, not something this package ships.
2. Looks that role up. If your org has no such role configured, the flow stops and returns an error message saying which role to add, because guessing would record the wrong relationship.
3. Works out the reverse role from the role's own Related Inverse (Parent flips to Child); a symmetric role like Spouse, Sibling or Friend is its own inverse.
4. Looks for each direction that may already exist, so their Ids ride into the two field-map transforms; the start date of an existing relationship is preserved and its end date cleared, reactivating a lapsed relationship instead of restarting it.
5. Saves both directions together with one call to the packaged Upsert Record(s) Bypass Duplicate Rules action, which upserts on Id. The reverse direction has to be written here because Salesforce does not create it automatically for records written by automation.

WHERE IT FITS: The NPC Account engine calls this after Contact 2 (an additional family member on a form) converts, relating them to Contact 1. It is form-agnostic: use it in your own flows whenever two Contacts need relating by role name. It never writes logs; it returns HasError and ErrorMessage instead.</description>
    <environments>Default</environments>
    <formulas>
        <description>Created only when neither direction existed yet, Updated otherwise: decided by the same lookups that made the saves updates.</description>
        <name>ConversionStatusValue</name>
        <dataType>String</dataType>
        <expression>if(AND(IsBlank({!getExistingForward.Id}),IsBlank({!getExistingInverse.Id})),'Created','Updated')</expression>
    </formulas>
    <formulas>
        <description>The role for the other direction: the role's own inverse (Parent to Child), or itself when the relationship is symmetric (Spouse, Sibling, Friend).</description>
        <name>Inverse_Role_Id</name>
        <dataType>String</dataType>
        <expression>BLANKVALUE({!getPartyRoleRelationship.RelatedInverseRecordId}, {!getPartyRoleRelationship.Id})</expression>
    </formulas>
    <formulas>
        <description>The sentence the caller logs when the org has no Party Role Relation for this role: it names the role so the fix is obvious.</description>
        <name>Missing_Role_Message</name>
        <dataType>String</dataType>
        <expression>'No Party Role Relation named ' &amp; {!Role_Name} &amp; ' for Contact to Contact relationships; add one in Setup and reprocess.'</expression>
    </formulas>
    <formulas>
        <description>Everyday wording mapped to the role names an org actually configures; anything else is passed through unchanged.</description>
        <name>Role_Name</name>
        <dataType>String</dataType>
        <expression>CASE({!RelationshipName}, 'Mother', 'Parent', 'Father', 'Parent', 'Stepparent', 'Parent', 'Legal Guardian', 'Parent', 'Foster Parent', 'Parent', 'Spouse or Partner', 'Spouse', {!RelationshipName})</expression>
    </formulas>
